<section><grid spacing="medium"><grid-item colspan="27" vertical-align="center"><p role="eyebrow">Digital · preserved interface</p><h2>The document joins a network</h2><p>CERN’s preserved early Web page makes the shift visible: linked terms turn a reading surface into a set of possible routes.</p><p>Berners-Lee proposed the Web at CERN in 1989. CERN released the Web software into the public domain on 30 April 1993.</p><p><b>Design question:</b> where can the reader go next, and how will they return?</p><p role="caption"><a href="https://info.cern.ch/hypertext/WWW/TheProject.html">CERN · preserved first website</a> · <a href="https://home.cern/science/computing/the-birth-of-the-web/">Web history</a></p></grid-item><grid-item colspan="33" vertical-align="center"><img src="https://cdn-staging.gamma.app/QWjS6fBfpt/agent-uploads/izdz8Kx9jyx_YmD5wTHlS.png" source="image.upload" height="320pt" alt="CERN-preserved early Web page, rendered in a modern browser for this prototype; not a period browser screenshot."></img><p font-size="12pt" role="caption">CERN-preserved early Web page, rendered in a modern browser for this prototype; not a period browser screenshot.</p></grid-item></grid></section>